## Competing interventions vendored for evaluation (src/evalab/interventions/)

These are other people's anti-slop tools, vendored so this project's
evaluation harness can measure itself against them on the same prompts.
They are evaluation subjects, not dependencies: nothing in the gate, the
CLI, the dashboard or the MCP server reads them, and removing the
directory changes no behaviour except that `--compare` has nothing left
to compare against.

Each is used in full and unmodified, with its own LICENSE file beside it
under the same basename. All three are MIT.

- `stop-slop.md` -- Stop Slop, Copyright (c) Hardik Pandya.
  https://github.com/hardikpandya/stop-slop -- MIT.
  SKILL.md concatenated with its own references/phrases.md,
  references/structures.md and references/examples.md, so the comparison
  is against the whole skill rather than the summary a progressive
  loader reads first.
- `no-ai-slop.md` -- No AI Slop, Copyright (c) Peter Yang.
  https://github.com/petergyang/no-ai-slop -- MIT.
  skills/no-ai-slop/SKILL.md.
- `anti-slop-writing.md` -- Anti-Slop Writing, Copyright (c) Ade Naufal.
  https://github.com/adenaufal/anti-slop-writing -- MIT.
  english/system-prompt.md.

Deliberately NOT vendored: jalaalrd/anti-ai-slop-writing. It ships no
license file, which reserves every right, so it cannot be included here
whatever its star count.

## Vale rule fixtures for the importer's own tests (src/core/testdata/vale-microsoft/)

Five unmodified rules from the Vale-compatible Microsoft Writing Style
Guide package, with that package's LICENSE beside them.

- Copyright (c) 2018 - 2019 Joseph Kato.
  https://github.com/vale-cli/Microsoft -- MIT.
  `Auto.yml`, `Contractions.yml`, `GenderBias.yml`, `Jargon.yml`,
  `Wordiness.yml`.

They are TEST FIXTURES, not shipped rules: nothing in the gate, the CLI,
the dashboard or the MCP server reads them, and no check in this
repository is derived from them. They exist because every other test of
the importer uses hand-written YAML that matches whatever the parser
expects, which is how a parser passes its own tests and fails on real
files.

## Vale rule imports

`stopslop.py import --vale` converts a Vale style package into custom
checks under `.claude/stopslop/custom_checks/`. Nothing from any Vale
package is vendored in this repository -- the command reads a directory
the user supplies. A project that imports someone's rules takes on that
package's own license for the checks it generates and must add its own
NOTICE entry for them. The command was verified against
krishnasunkam/vale-ai-tells (MIT), cited here as the test subject and
not redistributed.
